  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nToday will feature seasonably warm temperatures across NE Montana\naccompanied by isolated to widely scattered afternoon and evening\nthunderstorms with a flat ridge over the Northern High Plains.\nThis will allow for Pacific Northwest smoke to spread further into\nthe region. Chances for any specific location to receive any \nshowers or storms through tomorrow is relatively low (<20%) but \ncannot be completely ruled out.\n\nToward the middle of the week a potentially significant pattern\nshift is expected to take place for northeast Montana with an\nupper trough moving into the Canadian Rockies. This shift will \nlead to increasing daily chances for showers and thunderstorms, \nwith heavy rain potential as some southwestern monsoonal moisture \ndoes transport its way this far north. There will also be a major \ncooling with this pattern, where highs will be in the 60s and 70s \nfor many places as opposed to the current 80s to 90s.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nGenerally left the base forecast ride today with little\nadjustments.\n\nConfidence in any precipitation amounts are increasing even with\nprecipitation amounts continuing to climb for many areas, but \nthis still looks to be a classic setup for a low- \nconfidence/high- impact precipitation event where many locations \ncould get north of 1\" of total rainfall Thursday and Friday. \nBeyond the rain, confidence is moderate in the big temperature \nswing associated with the major pattern shift that could bring the\naforementioned rain. Once the system moves off to the east, \nconfidence goes down with respect to whether or not the region \nreturns to warm and dry, or if chances for rain will persist into \nthe weekend and next week.",
